-We further evaluate model generalization under an out-of-distribution (OOD) physical distribution shift across the transverse-field Ising phase boundary ($n=8$, $N_{\mathrm{tr}}=40$, $N_{\mathrm{te}}=100$, 20 seeds). Training ensembles are drawn exclusively from the paramagnetic regime ($h_i \in [0.8, 1.5]$), while test ensembles are drawn from the ferromagnetic regime ($h_i \in [0.2, 0.7]$).
-
-Due to the fundamental qualitative shift in ground-state entanglement structure and the accompanying shift in mean target values between phases, all evaluated models yield negative test coefficient of determination values ($R^2 < 0$), rendering $R^2$ uninformative for discriminating model performance. We therefore assess generalization using Mean Absolute Error (MAE), summarized in Table~\ref{tab:ood}. Full fidelity kernels (FQ) achieve a mean OOD MAE of $0.0602 \pm 0.0085$, compared to $0.1850 \pm 0.0294$ for PQK-2nn, resulting in a mean paired difference of $\Delta\mathrm{MAE} = -0.1248 \pm 0.0334$. A retrospective paired Wilcoxon signed-rank test on the OOD MAE difference yields $W=21.0$, raw $p=8.51\times 10^{-4}$ (Holm-adjusted $p_{\mathrm{Holm}}=8.51\times 10^{-4}$ within a retrospective three-comparison family including Train-Mean and Train-Median baselines), with FQ achieving lower MAE in 16 of 20 seeds.
-
-To confirm that models extract genuine inductive physical signal rather than trivial constant predictions, we evaluated baseline controls. Evaluating full FQ against the post-fit intercept-only ablation ($f(x) = b$, retaining the fitted SVR intercept while setting dual kernel weights $\boldsymbol{\alpha} = \mathbf{0}$) confirms that active kernel features reduce prediction error by $-0.4014 \pm 0.0156$ MAE across 20/20 seeds ($W=0.0$, raw $p=1.91\times 10^{-6}$). Evaluating against the zero-parameter training-mean constant predictor ($\hat{y} = \bar{y}_{\mathrm{train}}$) yields an error reduction of $-0.5459 \pm 0.0075$ MAE across 20/20 seeds ($W=0.0$, raw $p=1.91\times 10^{-6}$). These constant-control comparisons establish improvement over the evaluated constants rather than demonstrating a unique physical mechanism. Linear Ridge regression on two-body observables is reported for descriptive calibration ($0.1345 \pm 0.0136$), without confirmatory hypothesis testing; the full all-pairs classical $E(\text{raw})$ RBF baseline was not evaluated in this OOD experiment. Crucially, this OOD evaluation was performed on exact, noiseless statevectors without finite-shot sampling; whether the observed fidelity-kernel extrapolation advantage survives measurement noise or physical decoherence channels remains an open question. This benchmark is classified as exploratory.
+We further evaluate model generalization under an out-of-distribution (OOD) transverse-field distribution shift ($n=8$, $J=1.0$, $N_{\mathrm{tr}}=40$, $N_{\mathrm{te}}=100$, 20 seeds). Training ensembles are drawn from a higher-field regime ($h_i \in [0.8, 1.5]$), while test ensembles are drawn from a lower-field regime ($h_i \in [0.2, 0.7]$).
+
+Under this shift in transverse-field distribution and target values, all evaluated models yield negative test coefficient of determination values ($R^2 < 0$). While test $R^2$ is retained in Table~\ref{tab:ood} for completeness, Mean Absolute Error (MAE) serves as the primary metric for comparing out-of-distribution performance. Reported uncertainty values denote sample standard error ($\mathrm{SE} = s/\sqrt{20}$) across the 20 seeds, distinct from bootstrap confidence intervals reported elsewhere. Full fidelity kernels (FQ) achieve a mean OOD MAE of $0.0602 \pm 0.0085$, compared to $0.1850 \pm 0.0294$ for PQK-2nn, resulting in a mean paired difference of $\Delta\mathrm{MAE} = -0.1248 \pm 0.0334$. A retrospective paired Wilcoxon signed-rank test on the OOD MAE difference yields $W=21.0$, raw $p=8.51\times 10^{-4}$ (Holm-adjusted $p_{\mathrm{Holm}}=8.51\times 10^{-4}$ within a retrospective three-comparison family including Train-Mean and Train-Median baselines), with FQ achieving lower MAE in 16 of 20 seeds.
+
+To evaluate performance relative to simple baseline benchmarks, we compared against constant predictors. Evaluating full FQ against the post-fit intercept-only ablation ($f(x) = b$, retaining the fitted SVR intercept while setting dual kernel weights $\boldsymbol{\alpha} = \mathbf{0}$) shows an error reduction of $-0.4014 \pm 0.0156$ MAE across 20/20 seeds ($W=0.0$, raw $p=1.91\times 10^{-6}$). Evaluating against the training-mean constant predictor ($\hat{y} = \bar{y}_{\mathrm{train}}$) yields an error reduction of $-0.5459 \pm 0.0075$ MAE across 20/20 seeds ($W=0.0$, raw $p=1.91\times 10^{-6}$). These constant-control comparisons establish improvement over the evaluated constant predictors rather than demonstrating a unique physical mechanism. Linear Ridge regression on two-body observables is reported for descriptive calibration ($0.1345 \pm 0.0136$), without confirmatory hypothesis testing; the full all-pairs classical $E(\text{raw})$ RBF baseline was not evaluated in this OOD experiment. Crucially, this OOD evaluation was performed on exact, noiseless statevectors without finite-shot sampling; whether the observed fidelity-kernel extrapolation advantage survives measurement noise or physical decoherence channels remains an open question. This benchmark is classified as exploratory.
 
